.TH CLEAR 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
clear \- clear the terminal screen
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clear
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clear
writes two escape sequences to standard output:
.RB ESC\  [H ,
which moves the cursor to the top left corner of the screen, then
.RB ESC\  [J ,
which erases from the cursor to the end of the screen.
It writes these 6 bytes even when standard output is not a terminal.
.PP
When standard output is a console with virtual terminal processing
enabled, the console interprets the sequences; see
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE .
Otherwise busybox emulates them through the console API: it moves the
cursor to the top left corner of the console window and fills the window
from there to the bottom with spaces.
Either way only the visible window is cleared, and text that has
scrolled above it stays in the scrollback.
.PP
When standard output is not a console, such as a file or a pipe, the
bytes are written unchanged, for whatever reads them to interpret.
.SH OPTIONS
.B clear
takes no options, and ignores its arguments unless the first is
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
These variables are read only when standard output is a console.
.TP
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
How busybox handles escape sequences written to a console.
With
.B 0
or
.BR 2 ,
it disables the console's virtual terminal processing and emulates the
sequences itself.
With
.B 1
or
.BR 3 ,
it enables virtual terminal processing and passes the sequences
through, even if the console cannot interpret them.
With
.BR 4 ,
it passes them through if the console already has virtual terminal
processing enabled, and otherwise emulates them.
With
.BR 5 ,
it enables virtual terminal processing if the console supports it, and
otherwise emulates the sequences.
A number outside 0 to 5 means
.BR 5 ,
and text that is not a number means
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
Used when
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
is unset:
.B 1
means mode
.BR 1 ,
.B 2
means mode
.BR 5 ,
and any other value means mode
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B CONEMUPID
When neither of the above is set, the mode is
.BR 5 ,
or
.B 0
if
.B CONEMUPID
is set, as under ConEmu, or when running under Wine.
.PP
The busybox startup code also reads
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
and console output reads
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.BR LANG ,
none of which changes what
.B clear
does; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Standard output could not be written, as when it is closed.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited.
.SH EXAMPLES
Clear the screen:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ clear
.EE
.RE
.PP
Clear the screen before a build, so that only its messages show:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ clear && make
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save the sequence in a variable, and clear the screen with it later
without running
.BR clear :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cls=$(clear)
$ printf %s "$cls"
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the ncurses
.BR clear :
.IP \(bu 2
It writes the same sequence regardless of
.BR TERM ,
and does not use terminfo.
.IP \(bu 2
Like ncurses
.BR "clear \-x" ,
it never clears the scrollback.
.IP \(bu 2
It has no
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-V ,
or
.B \-x
options, and ignores other arguments instead of rejecting them.
.PP
To also erase the scrollback of a classic console window, use
.BR reset ,
which clears the console's whole screen buffer.
.PP
At an interactive
.B sh
prompt, Ctrl-L clears the screen the same way, keeping the command line
being edited.
.PP
The kit's
.B sh
runs
.B clear
inside the shell process, without starting a new one, except where the
shell needs a separate process anyway, as for a command substitution or
a pipeline.
When it runs inside the shell and standard output is a pipe whose
reader has exited, it ends the shell itself with status 141.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR reset (1),
.BR sh (1)
